{"assignees":[],"body":"Found while implementing the check-in slice of #77. This is a reading of the source that still needs confirming against running code — the state it describes is reachable today, but its consequence is not yet visible to a customer.\n\n## Where\n\n`app/actions.ts:1055-1061` — `changeBookingSeatsAction` applies each change with\n\n```ts\nawait tx.seatAssignment.updateMany({\n    where: { passengerId: change.passengerId, legId: change.legId },\n    data: { seatNumber: change.seatNumber }\n});\n```\n\n## What was observed\n\n`SeatAssignment.checkedInAt` (added in `20260818120000_record_traveller_check_in`)\nlives on the same row the seat change updates. The update rewrites `seatNumber`\nand leaves `checkedInAt` untouched, so a traveller who has checked in can move\nseats afterwards and stays checked in against a seat number that is no longer the\none they checked in for. Nothing records that the two disagreed.\n\nKeeping the check-in across a seat change is deliberate and correct — that is why\n`checkedInAt` sits on this row rather than in a table of its own, and why the seat\nchange is an in-place update rather than a new row. The gap is that nothing\nreacts to it.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nHarmless right now: no boarding pass exists yet, so there is no artefact carrying\na stale seat number, and the check-in page reads the seat live from the same row.\nIt stops being harmless the moment #77 generates a boarding pass — a pass issued\nat check-in and a seat changed afterwards is exactly the \"card names one flight\nwhile showing another leg data\" class of defect this codebase has shipped before\n(#137, #253), except printed and taken to an airport.\n\nThe boarding-pass slice has to decide one of: refuse a seat change once checked\nin, re-issue the pass on change, or record the change against the check-in so the\npass can be invalidated. Filing it now so the decision is made rather than\ninherited.","comments":[],"labels":[{"id":"LA_kwDOOKAPdM8AAAA